  One thing blocks me before I can write anything:

  [1mThe fork: there is no [38;5;153muserId[39m at the call site.[0m The submit handler reads only [38;5;153m#username[39m and [38;5;153m#password[39m from the form
  ([38;5;153mapp.js:19-20[39m), and [38;5;153mlogin[39m currently returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m. Adding a parameter is easy; the real
  question is what value flows into it, and the three plausible answers produce different interfaces.

  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m [3mis[0;1m the username[0m — then no new parameter is needed at all; [38;5;153mlogin[39m already knows who logged in, and the work is
    purely in what gets tracked. Cheapest, and nothing to undo later.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m is a client-generated correlation id[0m (e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m per attempt) — lets you tie a login attempt to
    later events before the server has authenticated anyone. Adds a parameter the caller must mint. Cheap to change
    now, annoying once anything downstream parses the id format.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m comes from outside the form[0m — a tenant/account/customer id you already hold in the page. This is the one that
    needs new plumbing: something has to put that id where the handler can read it. Most expensive, hardest to reverse,
    because it changes what the page has to know before login runs.

  There's also a sharp edge worth naming regardless of which you pick: identifiers in [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m are client-side and
  visible in the browser console, so whatever you pass shouldn't be anything you'd mind a user reading.

  My recommendation is the first. "Track who logged in" is already satisfiable with the [38;5;153musername[39m the function receives —
  adding a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter that the only caller would populate with the username buys a wider signature and no
  information. If you need the tracking to survive past the console, that's a separate, worthwhile change I'd rather
  scope on its own.
